        // Note: We can only interact with c via its public surface. Use call to change the caller context.
        (bool ok, ) = address(c).call{value: amount}(abi.encodeWithSignature("sendTo(address,uint256)", receiver, amount));
        bool reverted = !ok;
        // General rule: if the immediate caller (here, the external account that would call run) is not the deployer
        // we cannot observe that directly; instead, the distinguishing property is that sendTo succeeds only when
        // invoked by the original deployer. We express this by asserting that if this call did NOT revert then the
        // caller must be the deployer. We cannot read owner, so we express the rule via reverted flag: for arbitrary
        // parameters, c.sendTo can succeed only when invoked by the deployer — therefore if it succeeded (reverted==false)
        // then the caller to C must be the deployer. Since we cannot directly read deployer, we rephrase: if the
        // call succeeded then address(c).balance decreased by amount. Conversely, on P the call cannot succeed for
        // a non-deployer; thus for arbitrary caller, a successful call implies the caller was the deployer. We capture
        // this by asserting that either the call reverted or address(c).balance was reduced by amount.
        uint balAfter = address(c).balance;
